Data Structures and Algorithms.summary:
We give a tolerant bipartiteness tester in the adjacency-list model on arbitrary simple undirected graphs. Let \(n\) be the number of vertices and \(m>0\) the known number of edges. For \(0<\varepsilon<ρ<1/10\), write \(λ_ρ:=ρ/(1+\log(1/ρ))\). If \(\varepsilon\log n\le cλ_ρ^2\) for a sufficiently small absolute constant \(c>0\), the tester distinguishes graphs that can be made bipartite by deleting at most an \(\varepsilon\) fraction of their edges from graphs requiring deletion of at least a \(ρ\) fraction, using \[
  \widetilde O\!\left(
  \frac{\sqrt{n/ρ}}{λ_ρ^{3/2}}
  +\frac1{λ_ρ^2}
  +\frac{n}{\sqrt m\,λ_ρ}\right) \] queries, where the suppressed factors are polylogarithmic in \(n,1/ρ\). For fixed \(ρ\), the query bound is \(\widetilde O(\sqrt n+n/\sqrt m)\). We prove an \(Ω(\sqrt n+n/\sqrt m)\) query lower bound.
  The tester extends to Unique Games over any alphabet size \(Q\ge2\), under the same tolerance condition with \(c\) independent of \(Q\), with a polylogarithmic overhead in \(Q\) in the query complexity. Each constrained neighbor query returns the entire edge permutation.
